  "narrative": "The analysis examined whether late\u2011night phone use relates to charitable giving. Average donation amounts varied little across night\u2011use bands: occasional users gave \u00a321.59 (n\u202f=\u202f4\u202f535), regular users \u00a322.42 (n\u202f=\u202f4\u202f045), rare users \u00a322.04 (n\u202f=\u202f3\u202f145), and heavy users \u00a320.46 (n\u202f=\u202f710). The spread between the highest and lowest means is under \u00a32, indicating minimal difference. The correlation between the number of late\u2011night phone\u2011use sessions and donation amount was essentially zero (\u20110.0009, p\u202f=\u202f0.81, n\u202f=\u202f72\u202f000). In a regression model adjusting for income, the coefficients for night\u2011use bands were small and non\u2011significant: occasional\u202f=\u202f\u20110.1266 (p\u202f=\u202f0.241), regular\u202f=\u202f\u20110.0736 (p\u202f=\u202f0.505), heavy\u202f=\u202f\u20110.2515 (p\u202f=\u202f0.184). Income bands showed strong, significant effects (e.g., >\u00a380k\u202f\u2248\u202f\u00a35.99 higher than the <\u00a320k reference). Overall, there is no evidence of an association between late\u2011night phone use and charitable giving.",
